Usage: dircolors [OPTION]... [FILE]

Output commands to set the LS_COLORS environment variable.

Determine format of output:
  -b, --sh           output Bourne shell code to set LS_COLORS (default)
  -c, --csh          output C shell code to set LS_COLORS
  -p, --print-database  output defaults
      --print-ls-colors  output fully escaped colors for display

If FILE is specified, read it to determine which colors to use for which
file types and extensions.  Otherwise, a precompiled database is used.

Note: On Windows, dircolors outputs color codes compatible with ls --color.

OPTIONS:
  -b, --sh               output Bourne shell code to set LS_COLORS
      --bourne-shell     output Bourne shell code to set LS_COLORS
  -c, --csh              output C shell code to set LS_COLORS
      --c-shell          output C shell code to set LS_COLORS
  -p, --print-database   output defaults
      --print-ls-colors  output fully escaped colors for display
      --help             display this help and exit
  -V, --version          output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
